      "description": "**The Great Agent Hackathon, presented by TGPF 2026**\n\nThe Great Agent Hackathon is a 24-hour, in-person build happening overnight in Bangalore, as part of The Great Product Festival, India's largest product and startup conference.\n\nTeams get real problem statements, real mentorship through the night, and a live Main Stage finale in front of India's top product leaders at TGPF.\n\nThis year's hackathon is powered by Freshworks as title sponsor, with ElevenLabs powering the build.\n\n**Problem Statement Tracks**\n\n**• Track 1 — Customer Experience & Employee Onboarding:** agents built on Freshworks Agent Studio, MCP, and multi-agent orchestration.\n\n**• Track 2 — Platform Agent Skills & Knowledge:** reusable skills, MCP integrations, and the Freshworks developer platform.\n\n**• Track 3 — AI-native Enterprise (Open):** open canvas, any stack, greenfield agentic products.\n\n**Requirements**\n\n***Stage 1: Online Qualification Round***\nTo qualify for The Great Agent Hackathon, teams must submit the following on Devpost before the qualification deadline:\n\n1. **A Figma prototype or working demo** of your idea addressing one of the 3 problem statement tracks. This does not need to be a finished product, a clickable prototype, wireframe flow, or early build is enough to show the idea clearly.\n\n2. **A short written submission** covering:\n\n(i). The problem statement your team is addressing\n(ii). Your proposed solution and how it works\n(iii). Why your team is the right one to build it\n\n3. **A brief video walkthrough** (1-2 minutes) explaining your prototype and idea. Loom, YouTube, or Vimeo links are fine.\n4. **Team details:** names, roles, and one line on relevant experience for each team member.\n\nSubmissions will be reviewed by the TGPF and sponsor team. Shortlisted teams will be notified and invited to Bangalore for the in-person build.\n\n \n\n**Stage 2: In-Person Build (Bangalore, Shortlisted Teams Only)**\nShortlisted teams travel to Bangalore for the 24-hour overnight build as part of TGPF 2026.\n\nAt this stage, teams must submit:\n\n1. **A working prototype or demo** of the product built during the 24-hour build window, functional and ready to demo live.\n\n2. **An updated project description** covering what was built, the tech stack used, and how it evolved from the original Stage 1 idea.\n\n3. **Public code repository link** (GitHub or equivalent) with a clear README.\n\n4. **A live demo** on the Main Stage in front of judges and the TGPF audience.\n\n**Judging Criteria**\n\n**Innovation & Originality**\nHow creative and original is the idea? Does it approach the problem statement in a way we haven't seen before, or push beyond an obvious solution?\n\n**Technical Execution**\nHow well is the project built? Judges will look at functionality, code quality, and whether the team pulled off what they set out to build within the time given.\n\n**Use of AI / Agentic Design**\nDoes the project meaningfully use AI or agent-based workflows to solve the problem, rather than bolting AI on as an afterthought?\n\n**Relevance to Problem Statement**\nHow directly does the solution address the chosen track, Tech in Product, Growth and Monetisation, Sponsor-Driven Challenge, or Open Track, and how well does it solve a real, clearly defined problem?\n\n**Presentation & Demo Quality**\nHow clearly does the team communicate what they built, why it matters, and how it works during the live demo? Judges will weigh clarity and storytelling, not just polish.\n\n**Potential Impact**\nIf this were taken further, how much real-world value could it create for the people or businesses it's meant to serve?",
